# Experiment SOP (portable)

A generalizable standard operating procedure for AI-assisted research experiments. Drop this file into any project and reference it from that project's `CLAUDE.md` (e.g. "Follow `worklog/experiment_SOP.md` for all experiment work"). Written for Claude Code, but the roles are model-agnostic.

## Roles (three-model separation of duties)

    15	
    16	- **Controls** (probe, every arm): fresh k = 0 repeat, fresh k = 128 repeat and k = 512 (full turn) reproduce their originals exactly — max |Δ| = 0 on waveforms, log-spectrograms and acoustic metrics.
    17	- **Replication of exp_03** (same manifests, seeds and batch shape): `released_k8`, `control_k8` and `cyl_k8` replicate exp_03's GPU sweeps on all 6 337 queries at every angle with zero difference on EDT / C50 / T60 and the loss terms and ≤ 1.5e-7 on the log-spectrogram consistency, with identical validity masks (probe and full split; `generated/runs/<arm>/parity_exp03_{probe,all}.json`, every cell status `replication`). `released_k1` has no predecessor (controls only).
    18	- **Offline reproduction:** `check_online` OK on every run — the waveform and acoustic gaps recomputed from the saved float32 arrays equal the online values exactly; the spectral gaps are online-only by contract.
    19	- **Backend sensitivity:** the released K = 8 arm was first run on CPU (405 min; both GPUs were occupied). Its acoustic cells pass exp_03's per-query tolerances (criteria b, c, identical masks), but 7–9 of 6 337 queries differ from the GPU values in log-spectrogram MAD by up to 0.0095 (criterion a, ≤ 1e-5 per query, fails), so by the pre-registered rule (plan §7, v3.1) the CPU run is a **separate CPU protocol** — it did not replicate exp_03 as a whole — and it is kept apart from the four primary arms; the arm was re-run on GPU 1 once the card was free and the GPU run is the primary. The side-by-side table (`generated/backend_sensitivity_released_k8.md`) shows small point differences (largest relative difference 0.46 %, e.g. EDT 45° Δ −0.5848 ms GPU vs −0.5821 ms CPU; intervals overlapping to the third digit) and the same query-level denominator status in all 16 EDT / C50 / T60 / T60-absolute cells. This is a measured similarity on one arm, not backend equivalence. (Both CPU-side launcher failures, the K = 1 hash bug and the edited-while-running queue script are disclosed in the worklog; none touched a run's data.)
    20	- **Convergence:** every interval was recomputed with seed 1 (10 metrics × 4 angles × 4 arms = 160 non-zero-angle cells). Two headline cells are withheld as "unresolved Monte Carlo uncertainty", for two different reasons: `cyl_k8`, T60, 270° (the ratio bound moved by 0.109 of its width, above the 0.10 limit; both seeds say "improvement") and `control_k8`, T60-absolute, 45° (seed statuses disagree: "undefined" under seed 0, whose draws contain exact-zero Δ* at a fraction of 1e-4, vs "denominator uncertain" under seed 1). The tables disclose both; the figures' per-angle abbreviation is corrected in the round-8 regeneration to `mc?` for these cells (before it showed the query-level status).
    21	- **Pipeline qualification:** waveform and acoustic gaps measure the sensitivity of the model-plus-Griffin-Lim pipeline conditional on the per-query phase seed; Griffin-Lim can amplify a magnitude change. The GL-free spectral readout is reported beside them everywhere.
    22	
    23	## 3. Findings (descriptive; statuses and wording per plan §1 / §12)
    24	
    25	**3.1 The prediction moves a lot under a conditioning rotation — every arm, every angle.** Released K = 8: EDT gap 23.8–26.3 ms, C50 gap 0.59–0.64 dB, T60 gap 3.1–3.8 % (18–22 ms absolute), log-spectrogram MAD 0.157–0.180, magnitude rel-L2 0.20–0.23, waveform rel-L2 0.82–0.88; the query-level gap intervals are narrow (about ± 5 %), the room-cluster intervals are much wider (released K = 8 EDT 45°: 26.2 ms [10.6, 43.7]). The exp_01 models shift less (control: EDT 10.1–16.4 ms, C50 0.30–0.44 dB, T60 1.5–2.5 %; cylindrical: EDT 7.8–14.3 ms, C50 0.23–0.38 dB, T60 1.2–2.3 %); the released checkpoint evaluated at K = 1 (trained at K = 8) lies between them on EDT (10.6–12.5 ms) and is lowest on C50 / T60 at 45°, 90° and 270° (0.21–0.23 dB, 1.3–1.5 %) but not at 180°, where its C50 shift peaks (0.245 dB) and the cylindrical model is lower (0.235 dB, T60 1.23 % vs 1.44 %). For the K = 8 arms the shift is largest at 45° on every metric (the diagonal heading, as in exp_03) and similar at 90°, 180° and 270°.
    26	
    27	**3.2 The net accuracy change is small and, for the headline arm, mostly not distinguishable from zero — or an improvement.** Released K = 8 (query-level / room-level status): EDT Δ = −0.6 ms [−1.8, +0.6] at 45° and −0.9 [−2.0, +0.1] at 270° (denominator uncertain / uncertain), −1.6 [−2.7, −0.5] at 90° and −1.2 [−2.4, −0.06] at 180° (improvement at the query level; room level uncertain); C50 Δ = +0.033 dB [+0.006, +0.060] at 45° (defined / uncertain), −0.003, +0.008, +0.022 dB elsewhere (uncertain / uncertain); T60 Δ = +0.47 pp [+0.32, +0.62] at 45° (defined / defined), +0.26 at 90° and +0.16 at 270° (defined / uncertain), −0.24 at 180° (improvement / uncertain).
    28	
    29	**3.3 Where a multiple is reportable, the shift exceeds the change by an order of magnitude.** Reportable cells (query-level status "defined", converged, seed statuses agreeing), with the room-level status beside each:
    30	
    31	| arm | metric, angle | Δ (query CI) | G (query CI) | G/Δ [CI] | room status |
