    47	**3.4 Improvements and deteriorations largely cancel — shown by the paired contributions, not inferred from the ratio.** For the released K = 8 arm at 45°, 50.4 % of queries get a worse EDT, 47.3 % a better one and 2.3 % are unchanged (3 195 / 2 998 / 144 of 6 337); the mean positive part of e_α − e_0 is +10.9 ms and the mean negative part −11.4 ms, netting to −0.6 ms; C50: 52.5 % worse, 47.5 % better, none unchanged, +0.29 / −0.26 dB → +0.03 dB; T60: 53.0 % worse, 46.5 % better, 0.6 % unchanged, +2.0 / −1.5 pp → +0.47 pp. The same holds at the other angles (fractions worse 47–53 %, unchanged 0–3 %). Individual predictions therefore change by roughly the size of the prediction shift, in both directions.
    48	
    49	**3.5 Cross-arm remarks (descriptive).** The cylindrical model shifts less than the same-budget control on every metric and angle — by 13–23 % on the EDT gap (14.3 vs 16.4 ms at 45°), 14–24 % on C50, 6.5–20 % on T60 and 10–16 % on the log-spectrogram MAD (0.114 vs 0.127 at 45°) — but its EDT error rises at all four angles (+0.4 to +1.15 ms, query-level defined; room-level defined at 45° and 90°), whereas the control's EDT change is defined only at 90° — consistent with exp_03's paired k = 0 / rotation results. The control's T60 error *improves* at 90°, 180° and 270° (−0.12 to −0.34 pp). At K = 1 the released checkpoint's C50 error rises at every angle (+0.009 to +0.047 dB, all query-level defined, room-level uncertain) while its EDT improves at 90° and 180°.
